WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.097
- Anyone who's involved in science to say,

00:00:05.097 --> 00:00:08.360
"You should never change your mind,"

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.550
doesn't understand what science is.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.520
- Dr. Fauci, thank you
so much for joining us

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.650
on the YouTube channel again.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.520
It's been a year since
you and I have spoken.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.070
We're doing it now in
person without masks on

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.400
thanks to the vaccine.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.260
How amazing is that?

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.210
- Indeed, it's really one, I think,

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.500
one of the most important contributions

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.300
in biomedical research

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.560
to so quickly and
effectively get a vaccine

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.520
that right now is performing
in the real world even better

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.020
than it did in the clinical trials

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.300
- So you feel more
confident in the vaccine now

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.710
than perhaps you did in December of 2020?

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:48.710
- Oh yeah, and the reason
is that in December, 2020

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.790
we had the results of the clinical trials

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.610
which were very impressive,
you know, 94%, 95%,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.550
but often is the case,

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.820
is that when you get into the real world

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.200
of how the vaccine performs outside

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.150
of the pristine conditions
of the clinical trials,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.300
it doesn't usually look as good

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:10.880
as it looks in the clinical trials.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.140
On the contrary, we're
seeing the opposite now,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.580
we're seeing that when you get data,

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.090
not only from the United States,

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.650
I mean, when I say data,

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.080
I'm talking about hundreds
of thousands of people

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.670
being followed up in
prospective cohort studies,

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.580
the data are at least as
good if not even better

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.810
not only against what we
call the wild type virus,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.140
namely the one that's the standard one,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.280
but when variants appear,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.420
the vaccine seems to do
quite well against them also.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.650
So it really is very good
news with regard to vaccines.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.010
- In this year, how do
you feel where we are

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.710
in our current battle with the pandemic?

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.740
- Over all, I think it's very positive

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.220
but one of the things that
we get concerned about

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.360
is declaring victory prematurely.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.500
Because even though if you
look at all of the parameters,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.290
particularly most recently
over the last month or two,

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.330
is that the seven day
average of daily cases

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:13.200
continues to come down and down.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.130
One of the issues with that is
if we see the numbers go down

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.930
and people say, "Well, then
why should I get vaccinated?

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.440
Everything seems to be going
well," that's a mistake.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.680
If you don't really get the
level down very, very low,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.310
there's always the concern
and the real concern,

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.220
not just theoretical,

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.220
that you could start
getting more infections.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.420
At least in this country,

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.230
we're in a very fortunate situation

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:45.370
that we have enough vaccines
to vaccinate everybody.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.740
When you look at other countries,

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.890
where they're literally
and figuratively dying

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:56.490
to get vaccinated because they
don't have enough vaccines.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.724
I mean, take India has two to 3%

00:02:59.724 --> 00:03:02.510
of their population is fully vaccinated.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.410
And about 11 to 12% have
received at least one dose.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.300
I mean, that is not good.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.260
- Absolutely, and I've heard
you say numbers 70 to 80%

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.210
might be a target that we're looking for

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.070
for individuals to be vaccinated

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.080
in order to reach this
level of herd immunity.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.188
Are you still confident in these figures?

00:03:22.188 --> 00:03:23.960
- One of the things we
want to re-emphasize

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.960
because people say,
"Well, you're changing."

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.130
We're not changing any numbers.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.560
We don't know what the
threshold of herd immunity is.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.093
We know what it is for measles.

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:38.510
We're estimating that it's
somewhere between 70 and 85%

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.700
for SARS-CoV-2.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.330
But we don't know for sure.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.670
When you don't know for sure,

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:46.320
the thing to do is just forge ahead.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.500
- Over deliver.
- Yeah!

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.770
Just get as many people
vaccinated as you possibly can.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.860
And when you get that level of protection

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.980
that turns out to be the
threshold of herd immunity,

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.480
you'll know it, because
you'll see the infection

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.870
is almost disappear when
you get it to that level.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:07.250
- Where does natural
immunity play into this?

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:08.710
Because the last time you and I spoke,

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:10.530
one of the major questions
we were answering,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.710
granted it was April, 2020 at the time,

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.350
was that we believed, and
I think we still believe,

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.680
that reinfection is very rare.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.650
- Yes, it is.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:23.580
- Where do those individuals
fall on that 70, 80% spectrum?

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.390
Do we factor them in into that number?

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.940
- Well, we're talking about vaccines now,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.300
when we talk about the president's goal.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.300
If you're talking about
this somewhat elusive term

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.030
of threshold of herd immunity,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:39.700
you put them on into the same bucket,

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.330
because if you have someone
who's been infected,

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.420
recovered has a good immune response,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.930
that person can be protected

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.700
for a considerable period of time.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:49.860
We still don't know.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.180
I mean, people ask, "How
could you not know?"

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.070
The reason we don't know

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:55.130
is because we've only been doing this

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.650
for about a year or more, you know?

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.970
I mean, the whole thing
has been about 16 months,

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:03.890
but the vaccine plus infected
people, we don't know.

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:08.890
We hope it's considerably
longer than six, once a year.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.940
The companies have said, "Well,
you probably gonna need it."

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.670
We don't know what's gonna be in a year.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.750
- Exactly, I've seen no evidence to show

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.580
that we need a boost.
- Exactly.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.230
- Yet, the Pfizer CEO...

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:20.630
You think that's a business
decision more than-

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.700
Well, I think what they want to do

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.450
is they want to be prepared,
and that's the reason why.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.960
And that's the reason why
we, the federal government,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.880
in collaboration with industry
are actually doing trials

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.240
to give boosts to people from
the original clinical trials

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:41.970
to determine how high up
you get their response,

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.500
how long it lasts.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.420
So we're anticipating the possibility,

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:51.300
may be the likelihood, but
certainly the possibility

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.430
of needing to boost people
at some time or other,

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.300
both for the durability of the response,

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:03.300
but also to get maybe a
variant-specific boost.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:06.210
However, the good news about that,

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.510
as I mentioned a little bit ago,

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.160
is that many of the variants,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.770
particularly the dominant
one for us right now is B117,

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.530
the vaccine works extremely well.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:22.040
A number of studies now
have looked at what it does

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.770
to some of the other variants.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.880
And although it diminishes
your effectiveness a bit,

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:30.190
it's still well within the realm

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.430
of some reasonably good protection.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.310
- Because ultimately our goal,
at least the way I view it,

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.000
with the COVID-19 vaccination

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.810
is really to target severe illness,

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.010
hospitalizations, and deaths.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.180
Because if we can control those numbers,

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:47.300
turning it into a perhaps
less lethal virus,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.970
more like the flu, it
becomes more manageable.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:51.169
Is that sort of-
- Absolutely.

00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:53.270
And see, and that's one of the things

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:56.410
that we're working with now,
trying to get a good handle

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:01.040
on what the right approach
would be with the variants.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.220
'Cause we are gonna
continue to see variants

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:07.240
until the rest of the world is
essentially well controlled,

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.310
which it won't be for a couple of years,

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.930
even if we push to get people that,

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.370
we want to get the rest
of the world vaccinated

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.490
as quickly as possible.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.220
And I believe we have, you
know, a moral responsibility

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.960
to do that not only
because we're a country

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.770
that has resources

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:26.280
but other of the rich
country should do that.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.520
But it's also when our own self-interest

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:33.160
because when you have a lot of
dynamics of viral replication

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.940
in whatever country in the
world, it will generate variants,

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.330
and when it does sooner or later,

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.730
because of the
interconnectivity in the world,

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:43.280
we're gonna see variance here.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.290
But one of the things that
we really want to nail down,

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:50.380
but it looks, as I mentioned,
it looks pretty good,

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.510
that if you get a high level of immunity

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.460
against the original
virus, it does spill over

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.760
to protection against
several of the variants.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.920
- For individuals who
are vaccine-hesitant,

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:04.020
they've seen CDC guidelines change

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.540
a lot of times rightfully so
because the evidence evolved

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.490
and we needed to make
different recommendations.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:10.810
What do you say to those individuals

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.310
who are vaccine-hesitant right now?

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:13.143
- Well, first of all,

00:08:13.143 --> 00:08:15.250
you don't want to be
pointing a finger at them

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.830
or making them feel guilty.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.830
You want to try and explain to them

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.400
why it's important for
them to get vaccinated

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.070
by giving them two things:

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.440
giving them all the facts that they need

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.400
to answer any questions that they have.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.810
Was it too quick?

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.060
No, we have an answer to that.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.210
It's the culmination
of decades of research

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.100
that got us there.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:40.710
Wasn't the 11 months
from January to December.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.030
Then they ask, well,
did you test it enough?

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:43.863
I want to wait and see.

00:08:43.863 --> 00:08:45.570
Well, hundreds and millions of people

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:46.900
have gotten the vaccine now.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:50.210
So we're past the time
of "let's wait and see."

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:53.840
The other thing is to make
it extraordinarily easy

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.420
for somebody to get vaccinated,

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:59.960
because there's some times
is a combination of hesitancy

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.220
and what they consider inconvenience.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.650
Remember back early on,

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:05.830
people were trying to negotiate computers

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:10.470
and getting an appointment
and this 1A group, 1B, 1C.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:11.860
That's gone now.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:16.660
Right now, the way it is
it's extraordinarily easy

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.240
to get a vaccine.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.680
You got 40,000 pharmacies that can do it.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.860
You have pharmacies where you
don't even need to call up

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:26.497
and make an appointment,
you just walk in and say,

00:09:26.497 --> 00:09:28.000
"I want to get vaccinated."

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.820
You have community vaccine
centers, you have mobile units.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.340
It's very easy to get
vaccinated right now.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:35.840
- Yeah. I work a lot

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.180
with Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.980
in pushing out some of the
information about the vaccine

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.587
to help individuals who are
perhaps vaccine-hesitant

00:09:43.587 --> 00:09:47.030
or just want more
information on the subject.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:49.480
They're also asking a repeated question

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:51.670
about vaccinations in their children.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:54.920
You know, Pfizer has the
approval at least the EUA

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.920
for 12 and up now.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.840
What do you say to someone
who's vaccine-hesitant

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.517
for their children when they're saying,

00:10:00.517 --> 00:10:02.540
"Well, you know, children
seem to experience

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.180
a milder case perhaps of COVID-1,

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:07.400
they're not perhaps
spreading it as often."

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:08.590
Should children be vaccinated?

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:10.110
- Yeah, I mean, there's no doubt.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.812
It's the chain of transmission.

00:10:11.812 --> 00:10:16.812
And even though children statistically

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:21.523
don't get as serious disease, they can.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.500
And if you look at the hospitalizations,

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:26.350
that's absolutely correct.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.650
Very low for the children,

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:28.760
then gets some more and more and more

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.690
as you go up for the elderly.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:35.530
But if you look now today,
where the overwhelming majority

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.613
of the elderly are protected,

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:43.510
I mean we now have, you know,
about 80% of the elderly

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:45.850
at least one dose.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.080
I mean, that's pretty good.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:48.510
And the hospitalizations

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.940
and deaths among the elderly
dramatically diminished.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:53.730
Then you're starting
to see, well, you know,

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:57.210
young people do get infected
and some of them do get sick.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:00.020
So it's, again, the health of the person.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:02.010
Even if you get mild disease,

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:06.830
kids, young people get long
COVID, there's no doubt.

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:08.690
That persistence of that

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:13.810
really sometimes almost
incapacitating symptoms of fatigue

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.210
and muscle ache and temperature,

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.270
dysregulation, things like that.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:19.600
And then there's the chain of transmission

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.890
that, you know, you'll see papers

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:24.650
that say kids don't transmit it....

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:26.340
Kids can transmit it.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:27.961
Anybody that says that they don't,

00:11:27.961 --> 00:11:29.590
they're not looking at reality.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:32.510
They may, at certain
levels, not transmit it

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:35.547
as efficiently as the crowding
that you get as you get older

00:11:35.547 --> 00:11:38.490
and the kind of things
that people do indoors,

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:40.780
but it is part of the
chain of transmitting.

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.930
- If you are vaccinated,
whether you're 50 years old,

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.330
20 years old, 65 years old,

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.380
what can you feel comfortable
doing once you are vaccinated?

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.250
- Again, there's a variability.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:55.260
I think people who are
significantly immunosuppressed,

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.880
transplant patients, people who are

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.970
on immunosuppressive therapy,

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:01.850
they may want to be a
little bit more careful

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:03.190
with regard to masks,

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:07.620
but the general average
person who is vaccinated

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.420
can for their own safety
do almost anything.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.610
I mean, indoors or outdoors.

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:17.610
The question is when you're
in a mixed environment,

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:19.810
where you're in an establishment,

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:22.910
there are people who were
vaccinated, people who were not,

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:24.920
that people who might be infected

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:26.700
that might be infecting others,

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:29.470
there's the question of what
is the establishment gonna do?

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:32.130
Are they gonna say, "If
you want to come into my,"

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:35.110
whatever the establishment
is, it could be a college,

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:37.170
you know, it could be a workplace.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.400
That person has almost
the right, if you want,

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:43.590
to say, if you want to do that
you've got to wear a mask.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:44.880
Which then it will have,

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.430
the vaccinated person will
say, "Well, wait a minute.

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:50.680
I'm vaccinated. Why do
I got to wear a mask?"

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:52.490
And that's where the confusion comes in,

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.150
which they're trying to sort out.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:59.150
- Once everyone is vaccinated
or the 70, 80% numbers are hit

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.290
for herd immunity, what happens?

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:02.667
Does COVID-19 go away?

00:13:02.667 --> 00:13:04.810
- It certainly will go to a level

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:08.560
that would no longer be
a public health hazard

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:10.940
as it were in this country.

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.360
But again, it's a global pandemic

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:15.910
and we do require a global response.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.750
- What level do you think
that we should start saying,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.740
you know, our supply is
bigger than demand here.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.400
We need to start taking our supply

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:24.520
and sharing it with the world.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:25.605
- We're gonna do it.

00:13:25.605 --> 00:13:27.520
By the end of the June, beginning of July,

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:31.740
we're gonna be giving out 80
million doses for a starter.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.420
Just for a starter. And
there's more coming after that.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.970
- As far as our handling of
the pandemic as a nation,

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:39.470
as a scientific community,

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:40.740
if you could change one thing

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.330
or make an improvement on one
thing on our response so far,

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:44.950
what would it be?

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:47.250
- Well, to me, as I've said often,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.780
it's that there's been
an extraordinary amount

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.480
of divisiveness in this country.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.690
That's the worst possible thing.

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:57.490
I mean, there's differences of opinion,

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:01.610
but the the intensity of the divisiveness

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:03.600
has in so many respects,

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.010
and one can't deny it, the politicization

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.620
of a public health approach.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:12.500
I mean, when someone does or
does not want to do things

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:14.710
like wear a mask, congregate setting,

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:16.800
for reasons that are not,

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.310
they don't make public health sense,

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:21.420
they're more ideologic than
they are public health.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.560
So if we were gonna do
something different.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.010
We could say, there's
differences of opinion,

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.750
there's differences of ideology, fine.

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:32.730
But when you're facing a
public health challenge

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:34.930
of this magnitude,

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:37.178
why don't we put that aside for a minute,

00:14:37.178 --> 00:14:39.100
take care of the pandemic,

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:41.610
then you could go back to
ideological differences.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:44.340
- We're talking about masking right now

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:46.960
with the pandemic and the
public health setting.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.570
I've heard you say in an interview before

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:53.463
that masking can potentially
continue past the pandemic-

00:14:53.463 --> 00:14:56.090
- That was taken completely out of context

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:59.150
by people that like to "gotcha!" you know?

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:01.670
No. What I was saying, I was...

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:02.960
Thank you. Let me clarify this.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.630
- I love it.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:06.540
- I was asked, people were saying,

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:10.850
you know, it's interesting: in Australia

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:15.650
during their flu season,
they had almost no flu.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:17.567
They called us up and said,

00:15:17.567 --> 00:15:20.600
"Wow, we think that's due to the fact

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.540
that we will wearing masks,

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:24.890
we were avoiding congregate settings

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.820
in order to prevent
the spread of COVID-19.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:33.680
And we had a secondary effect
that we almost had no flu

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:37.363
or other respiratory
illnesses, RSV, parainfluenza.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:41.050
We had almost nothing."

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:45.680
Then our winter came and
we noticed the same thing,

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:48.270
that wearing masks, avoiding
congregate settings,

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:49.350
et cetera, et cetera.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.087
So I was asked in an interview that

00:15:52.087 --> 00:15:55.930
"Do you think that some
people might elect,

00:15:55.930 --> 00:16:00.220
elect! might elect that they
might want to wear a mask

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:02.900
during the winter season to avoid flu

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:04.570
or other respiratory illnesses?

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.110
And I said, "You know, it seems reasonable

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:10.000
if some people feel that they
want that extra protection,

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:11.170
they could do it."

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:13.000
It's not going to be mandated.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.900
- My question is more so
not for that gotcha purpose,

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:19.153
but in terms of protecting
yourself, using a mask,

00:16:19.153 --> 00:16:21.540
you know in March of 2020, April, 2020,

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.380
even on my own YouTube channel
as a family medicine doctor,

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:28.010
I was urging people to not
rush to buy masks at that time,

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:29.840
because the information we had at the time

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:33.740
wasn't focused on asymptomatic
or presymptomatic spread.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.875
There were shortages of PPE.

00:16:35.875 --> 00:16:39.170
Like I was seeing patients
in March with no masks,

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:41.500
not because of inadequate PPE.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.840
but because we didn't think
that was mandatory. So-

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:47.020
- Yes. And that's what I was saying then.

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:48.970
And then when we found out three things,

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:50.260
I mean thank you for bringing that up

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:53.070
because that's when I
get accused by people

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:55.240
who want to do a gotcha,
that flip-flopping.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:56.080
There's no flip-flop.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.830
Back then there were three things:

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:02.250
A, we were told that there was
a shortage of PPE and masks,

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:03.083
and we felt, you know,

00:17:03.083 --> 00:17:05.090
we don't want to take it away from others.

00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:09.960
Secondly, there were no data
meta-analysis at the time

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:14.720
that masks were effective
outside of the hospital setting.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:17.790
And then three, importantly,
we didn't fully realize

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:21.450
how pervasive asymptomatic spread was.

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:22.950
So what changed?

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.950
A, was clear there was
no shortage of masks,

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:30.130
cloth masks work, meta-analysis
showed that, in fact,

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:33.360
even outside of the hospital
settings masks work.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:36.840
And third, we were hit
with the realization

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:40.000
that anywhere from 50
to 60% of transmissions

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.060
occur from asymptomatic people.

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:44.480
That's the reason why we went from saying

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.150
you don't really need to wear a mask

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.700
to you should really wear a mask.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:53.210
That's not flip-flopping,
that's taking evolution of data

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:56.080
and then making your
recommendations based on the data.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:57.840
- Yeah, in fact, if we
didn't make that adjustment,

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.450
that would be worse for us

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:01.690
because then we're not looking at the data

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:03.340
and evolving with the science.

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:08.293
- Well, anyone who's
involved in science to say

00:18:09.757 --> 00:18:12.950
"you should never change your mind,"

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:15.160
doesn't understand what science is.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.110
- You've become a champion of science.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:20.050
On social media you've become a meme.

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:24.610
Your face has been put on
t-shirts, socks, cupcakes.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:28.020
I've seen everybody celebrate
the work that you've done

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:30.660
leading the way with
humility, with honesty,

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:33.750
treating science as it
deserves to be treated.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:34.600
How does that feel?

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.670
- Well, I focus on what I'm
doing and not basking in that,

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:41.350
because that's balanced by
the other side of people

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:46.040
who attack, who are not science-related,

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:48.470
who are anti-science,

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.940
it comes from everything
from, you know, verbal attacks

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:57.940
to ad hominem nonsense, to
threats to my life and my family.

00:18:59.066 --> 00:19:00.480
So well, it's balanced.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:01.960
- How does that negativity affect you?

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:02.793
That's gotta be hard.

00:19:02.793 --> 00:19:04.860
I mean, as scientific communicators,

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:07.310
I mean, if you look over
the course of history,

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:09.960
scientific communicators,
weren't really celebrated.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.610
Researchers were almost frowned upon

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:13.900
when they would spend
a lot of time in media.

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:15.600
How do you balance that?

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.610
- No, I mean, right now
you have to communicate,

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:20.900
because there's an absolute
need on the public to know

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.570
and understand the truth.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:24.710
This is something that's
threatening their lives,

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:26.520
their family, the economy.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:28.110
When you politicize that,

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:31.680
when you have taken out of
the realm of public health

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:33.810
and make it become almost
a political statement,

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:37.080
that's very damaging
to the overall effort.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:40.550
And the other thing is that
if you want to really get into

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:43.190
and understand the evolving science,

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:45.920
you've got to realize that things change.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.860
And you've got to be humble
enough and flexible enough

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.110
that when you get new data,

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:55.500
that you then change what
you're doing based on the data.

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:58.090
If you say, "I really
want to never change,

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:00.270
because if you change your flip-flopping,"

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.970
then you're back here and
you don't take advantage

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:08.290
of the new data that every
week, every month comes by.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:11.840
That's the thing that some
people don't seem to understand.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.950
- Well, it's like the idea
that scientists can't be wrong.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.890
And that's part of being a
scientist, testing a hypothesis.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:19.470
But science itself is a process

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.250
that can never be wrong, right?

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:23.940
- As we've learned in medical school

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:26.298
when you do these kinds of things,

00:20:26.298 --> 00:20:28.710
the mentors always told us

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.480
that science by definition
is self-correcting.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:33.578
- You said that beautifully.

00:20:33.578 --> 00:20:36.364
I want to read to you a statement

00:20:36.364 --> 00:20:39.170
that one of my colleagues
shared with me late last night

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:40.010
when I mentioned to them

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:41.600
that I was gonna be interviewing you.

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:43.810
She's a cardiologist on the West Coast.

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:46.280
And I want just to read this to you

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:48.580
to highlight the impact that
you've had on individuals,

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.487
that perhaps you may not come
across in your everyday life.

00:20:51.487 --> 00:20:53.440
"I got redeployed to a COVID unit

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:56.530
for my last three months of
my cardiology fellowship.

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:59.440
I went to work every day
before we had adequate PPE.

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:02.720
And I was putting people
my age on ECMO daily.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:06.850
I was constantly worried I was
gonna die. I was so scared.

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:08.120
But I would come home from work

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.120
and see Dr. Fauci on television.

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:12.187
And he honestly made me less scared."

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:15.170
Hearing that, how do you feel?

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:18.780
- Well, that obviously
makes you feel good.

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.510
I mean, because that's what I do.

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:23.240
That's what my job is.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.320
I'm a physician, I'm a scientist,

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:29.790
trying to get as accurate
information out to people.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:33.870
So if I can make a
cardiology fellow feel good,

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:35.080
that makes me feel good.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:36.710
- You've been doing this for a while.

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:39.350
You've served under many presidents.

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:41.400
You've worked with administrations

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:44.540
of left, right, in between.

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:45.373
Is this the highlight?

00:21:45.373 --> 00:21:47.160
- We're still in the middle of it.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:50.340
I'll tell you that when it's over.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:53.000
We're still in the middle of
it. We still have a way to go.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:56.150
It looks like we're gonna
nail it. I think we are.

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:58.303
Science will come to the rescue.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:03.340
But yeah, I believe we're
gonna get out of this.

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:05.520
This is gonna end and it's gonna end

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.360
and hopefully we'll learn a good lesson

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:09.690
and be better off for it.

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:12.300
- For sure. So everyone
at home get vaccinated.

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:13.480
That's the goal.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:15.050
We need to get everyone vaccinated

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:16.300
so that we don't have to play the game

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:18.077
of who's vaccinated and who's not.

00:22:18.077 --> 00:22:19.110
(both laughing)

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.080
And on a final light-hearted note,

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:24.890
in 2015 People Magazine named
me sexiest doctor alive,

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:27.830
it's kind of the joke of how
I got started on social media.

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:30.580
And I use that as an
opportunity to go on television,

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.490
talk about primary care,
prevention, vaccinations.

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:36.500
Since then they haven't given
the title to anybody else.

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:38.770
In fact, before me, it
was Dr. Sanjay Gupta.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:39.670
- Right.

00:22:39.670 --> 00:22:43.830
- How would you feel about me
passing on that title to you?

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:44.896
- No, thank you.

00:22:44.896 --> 00:22:47.423
You're definitely more sexy than I am.

00:22:47.423 --> 00:22:49.829
You can keep it. I'm 80 years old.

00:22:49.829 --> 00:22:51.490
It's enough.

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:53.510
- I just think that would
be great culmination.

00:22:53.510 --> 00:22:55.340
You've done all this amazing work.

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:58.640
You've kept people like my
friend, Dr. Balardo calm

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:00.430
during a very stressful state.

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:02.470
You've given us the best information

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:03.660
with the most humble approach,

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:06.660
but I think it would be a
funny top internet story.

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:08.000
So maybe we can get People Magazine

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.060
to throw you on for 2021.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:12.600
- We'll see. Let's let's
get this over with first.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:14.447
- Thank you so much for
your time, Dr. Fauci,

00:23:14.447 --> 00:23:15.800
for all of your work.

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:17.880
Seriously, everyone from
the medical community,

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:21.250
my hospital, Atlantic Health
System, Senator Schumer,

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:23.350
everyone we've been
working with so tirelessly

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:24.390
to get people vaccinated,

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.050
and you're leading the
charge on that, so thank you.

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:28.347
- Thank you. Thank you for being here.

00:23:28.347 --> 00:23:30.930
(upbeat music)

